WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Cylinder transfilling: what actually happens when you connect two cylinders?

00:00:05.350 --> 00:00:14.677
On the left, you set up two cylinders: A is the source, B receives the gas. On the right, you see how full each one is.

00:00:14.827 --> 00:00:22.855
Cylinder A is a 12-litre single at 200 bar. Cylinder B is a 7-litre cylinder at 50 bar.

00:00:23.005 --> 00:00:35.683
The small line under each pressure is the gas inside, as litres at 1 bar: 12 l × 200 bar = 2400 l.

00:00:35.833 --> 00:00:42.285
Now open the valves. Gas flows from high pressure to low, until both pressures are equal.

00:00:42.435 --> 00:00:53.114
They meet at about 145 bar — not halfway at 125. The bigger cylinder pulls the result towards its own pressure.

00:00:53.264 --> 00:00:59.565
The rule is simple: add up the gas in both cylinders, then share it across the total volume.

00:00:59.715 --> 00:01:08.416
Now a big twinset, 2 × 12 litres at 200 bar, topping up a small 3-litre pony at 50 bar.

00:01:08.566 --> 00:01:15.345
The pony jumps to over 180 bar. The twinset barely notices the gas it gave away.

00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:26.775
This is the ideal-gas picture. In reality the receiving cylinder warms up as it fills, so its pressure drops a little once it cools.

00:01:26.925 --> 00:01:31.779
Try it yourself in the Deco Theory sandbox. Thanks for watching!
